WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.760
So you guys have heard about Dr Foutse Khomh&nbsp;
talking about how to find bugs from deep learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:21.660
programs, and I will then talk about how to figure&nbsp;
out what goes wrong - figure out the root causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:29.160
of the these bugs. So this says AI artificial&nbsp;
intelligence has been increasingly embedded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:35.700
into modern software applications and systems and&nbsp;
the key enabler behind this is deep learning. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.500
you know deep learning is quite different from&nbsp;
traditional software engineering. In traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:46.380
software development we programmers manually&nbsp;
write the program logic in source code, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:53.400
but in deep learning the models actually learn&nbsp;
their own logic from the training data. And also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:00.840
in traditional software development everything is&nbsp;
encoded in symbolic representations, right, data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:08.280
structures etc. etc. But in deep learning, things&nbsp;
are encoded into high-dimensional arrays and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.340
is a lot of calculus and linear algebra operations&nbsp;
over these arrays to make the inference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.260
This brings a lot of challenges&nbsp;
to debug deep learning models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.680
You know, in the old world, traditional software,&nbsp;
if we want to debug errors, we basically set a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.960
breakpoint and we can step through each line of&nbsp;
code to figure out the root cause of this error.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.000
But in deep learning, for neural&nbsp;
nets, how do we even set a breakpoint?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.600
Even if sometimes we can actually peek into&nbsp;
the internals, you know, a specific layer or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.400
neuron in the neural net, but what we can get&nbsp;
from at the runtime? The runtime values we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.560
from this neural net are high dimensional&nbsp;
arrays which are hard to interpret, right?&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.640
All right, let's actually look at one example.&nbsp;
Suppose you're a programmer and you actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:06.060
tried this recurrent neural network to classify&nbsp;
ready comments to positive or negative opinions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.460
and you find out the model accuracy is&nbsp;
pretty low, and you are wondering why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:17.460
right? So for example, this comment clearly&nbsp;
shares a negative opinion, but the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:24.300
misclassifies it as positive. So it's really hard&nbsp;
to tell exactly what happens inside the model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.880
right, and why this error occurs. And this&nbsp;
is actually why some people, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.640
actually call deep learning "alchemy", right?&nbsp;
People don't know exactly why something works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:41.820
and why something doesn't work. Okay, it's&nbsp;
like a giant black box to us to programmers.&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:49.140
Our solution is to transform this complex and&nbsp;
familiar neural network back to something we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.480
are familiar with, right, like, uh, finite state&nbsp;
machine, and in this way we can bring back all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.480
good stuff we have in the traditional programming&nbsp;
world, like, people can step through each state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:07.680
in the finite state machine and inspect the state&nbsp;
value, um, in the in the finite state machine. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.480
based on this idea we actually build a debugging&nbsp;
tool called the DeepSeer. In the middle of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.120
interface is a visualization of the finite&nbsp;
state machine abstracted from an RN model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:23.580
and it provides programmers a bird's eye view of&nbsp;
what's already going on in the internal decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:31.260
making process of the model. As the model reads&nbsp;
each word in input text it will transit between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:38.520
different state until it reach to the end of the&nbsp;
text to make it the final decision. And if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:43.920
click on state you can actually see the frequent&nbsp;
words and phrases associated with this state.&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.000
So in this way we convert those&nbsp;
uninterpretable high dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.740
arrays into a symbolic representation&nbsp;
that are interpretable to programmers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.700
And you can also dig into individual data points&nbsp;
in the training data, search and filter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:05.520
trace all the way back to the state diagram.
Going back to the previous misclassification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.520
example, with dips here you can actually enter&nbsp;
this misclassified text into DeepSeer and DeepSeer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:18.600
will immediately produce a state trace that&nbsp;
reflects the intermediate decisions made by the RN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:26.220
model after reading each word in the input text.&nbsp;
Here, red means negative and blue means positive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:33.000
Similar to how you step through a program the&nbsp;
source code you can step through this state trace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:39.900
and look at what happens after you know the model&nbsp;
reads each word in the input text. And we can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.460
that, you know, this RN model actually changes&nbsp;
minus several times when it's reading the input&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.800
text and in particular after, you know, it reads&nbsp;
this word quarters at the end of the sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:57.240
the model suddenly changes its mind from negative&nbsp;
to positive right and never change it back. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:01.740
it actually provides us some good hints for&nbsp;
further debugging - further investigation.&nbsp;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:08.580
When we actually search this keyword quarters in&nbsp;
the training data, right, we can see that the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:14.760
is quite imbalanced - there are 28 sentences&nbsp;
that mention these quarters and 27 of them are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:20.820
labeled as positive only one of them is labeled&nbsp;
as negative, so that means the model actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:26.580
learns a superficial correlation between&nbsp;
this world quarters and a positive opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.480
right? So this is very likely to be the&nbsp;
root cause of this misclassification.&nbsp;

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:37.140
To further confirm the usefulness of this&nbsp;
idea we actually did a user study with 28&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:42.420
CS students and asked them to debug two different&nbsp;
models - train on two different data sets using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:48.540
DeepSeer versus another popular debugging -&nbsp;
deep learning debugging tool called Line and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:53.400
we found that students using DeepSeer provided&nbsp;
more reasonable insights about the root causes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:59.100
And we also found more error-inducing keywords&nbsp;
in the input sentence compared with using Line.&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:06.000
So OK takeaways. You know, I think given the&nbsp;
opaqueness of deep learning, I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:12.540
quite important for us programmers to understand&nbsp;
what we are building and be responsible for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:18.600
model errors. And to achieve this goal we actually&nbsp;
need to build new debugging tools that account for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:24.960
the unique characteristics of deep learning.&nbsp;
And to make these tools usable to the broad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:30.600
population of programmers we need to transform the&nbsp;
internals of a deep learning model into something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:36.180
that we are familiar with that are interpretable&nbsp;
to programmers rather than something that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:42.660
makes sense to machine learning experts and&nbsp;
machine learning theorists. Finally I want to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:47.040
a shout out to my collaborators at the University&nbsp;
of Alberta and thank you all for listening.

